<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arch of Augustus (Susa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9–8 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arch of Augustus at Susa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was built in 9–8 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius">Marcus Julius Cottius</a> to mark his alliance with Augustus. Its inscription names Cottius as prefect and lists the Alpine peoples he governed. The marble arch, with reliefs of a sacrifice, stands near the road to the Montgenèvre pass and is one of the best-preserved Augustan monuments in northern Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arch-of-augustus-susa.jpg" alt="Arch of Augustus (Susa)" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Duvilar (Lorenzo Rossetti) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arco_di_Augusto-Susa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
